{
  "$comment": "JEDDs (Joint Economic Development Districts) and JEDZs (Zones) -- contractual arrangements letting a municipality tax income earned in adjoining UNINCORPORATED township land, without annexation. This is the exact mechanism flagged last session as breaking Michigan-style 'closed list, default zero' certainty for rural Ohio -- now enumerated by name and rate instead of left as an abstract caveat.",
  "year": 2026,
  "asOf": "2026-09-01",
  "source": {
    "title": "Ohio JEDD/JEDZ Income Tax Rate Database Table (The Finder, bulk CSV download)",
    "url": "https://thefinder.tax.ohio.gov/api/file-downloads/content?target=https%3A%2F%2Fapi.thefinder.tax.ohio.gov%2Ffinder%2Fapi%2Fv1%2Ftax-rates%2Fdownloads%2FJEDTaxRates.csv",
    "downloadedOn": "2026-09-01",
    "downloadedBy": "Re-fetched directly via curl (through the site's own same-origin proxy route -- the direct api.thefinder.tax.ohio.gov URL now 401s, see harvester/sources.json's oh-jedd-rates note) during a full re-check of all 176 rows. Same feed as the municipal rate table. Found and fixed two real gaps against the 2026-08-11 version: Miami Township-Dayton JEDD (id 9080) was still carrying its pre-2026 rate (1.75%, expired 2025-12-31) instead of the 2.25% rate effective 2026-01-01; and three new JEDDs effective 2026-05-01 (Jersey-New Albany JEDD 1/2/3, ids 9158-9160, all 2%) were entirely missing.",
    "rateFormat": "Source CSV stores rate as a raw percentage number (e.g. 1.8 meaning 1.8%), NOT a fraction -- unlike the municipal CSV's .02500-style fraction. Converted here by dividing by 100 to a fraction (0.018) for consistency with OH-municipalities-2026.json; rateSourceRaw preserves the original value so the conversion is checkable, not just asserted."
  },
